.TH GO-XPRA 1 "1 August 2026" "go-xpra 0.2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
go-xpra \- minimal Xpra client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B go-xpra
.br
.B go-xpra
.RI [ options ]
.BR tcp:// [ username [ : password ]@] host [ : port ]/
.br
.B go-xpra
.RI [ options ]
.BR ssl:// [ username [ : password ]@] host [ : port ]/
.br
.B go-xpra
.RI [ options ]
.BR ws:// [ username [ : password ]@] host [ : port ]/[ path ][? query ]
.br
.B go-xpra
.RI [ options ]
.BR wss:// [ username [ : password ]@] host [ : port ]/[ path ][? query ]
.br
.B go-xpra
.RI [ options ]
.BR ssh:// [ username [ : password ]@] host [ : port ]/[ display ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B go-xpra
connects to an Xpra server and displays its forwarded application windows on
the local desktop.
It supports plain TCP, TLS, WebSocket, secure WebSocket, and SSH connections.
.PP
When invoked without a URL,
.B go-xpra
opens a connection dialog for the protocol, credentials, host, port, and
protocol-specific path or display.
.PP
The default ports are 14500 for TCP and TLS, 80 for WebSocket, 443 for secure
WebSocket, and 22 for SSH.
TLS connections verify the server certificate and host name against the system
trust store by default.
.PP
SSH connections use the system
.BR ssh (1)
client and run
.B xpra _proxy
on the remote host.
The remote host must have
.B xpra
in its
.BR PATH .
OpenSSH configuration, identities, agents, jump hosts, and host key handling
apply normally.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-backend " " auto | x11 | wayland
Select the Linux display backend.
The default,
.BR auto ,
uses X11 when
.B DISPLAY
is set and otherwise tries Wayland when
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
is set.
This option is available on Linux only.
.TP
.BR \-\-ssl\-ca\-cert " " file
Add the PEM certificates in
.I file
to the system certificate authorities trusted for
.B ssl://
and
.B wss://
connections.
.TP
.B \-\-ssl\-insecure
Disable certificate and host name verification for
.B ssl://
and
.B wss://
connections.
This is intended only for local testing.
It cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-ssl\-ca\-cert .
.TP
.B \-v
Log packet and window event details.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the program version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print command usage and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XPRA_PASSWORD
Xpra protocol password to use when it is not present in the connection URL.
If this variable is unset and the server requests authentication,
.B go-xpra
prompts for the password.
.TP
.B XPRA_BACKWARDS_COMPATIBLE
Set to
.B 0
to accept only Xpra 6.5 and later packet types.
Legacy packet reception is enabled by default.
.TP
.B DISPLAY
X display used by the X11 backend.
Its presence also gives X11 priority when
.B \-\-backend auto
is used.
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
Wayland compositor socket used by the Wayland backend.
.TP
.BR USER ", " USERNAME
Local user name used when the connection URL omits one.
.TP
.B PATH
Used to locate
.BR ssh ,
.BR sshpass ,
and
.BR pinentry .
.SH AUTHENTICATION
For TCP, TLS, and WebSocket connections, a password omitted from the URL is
read from
.B XPRA_PASSWORD
or requested interactively.
On Linux,
.B pinentry
is tried first, followed by a hidden terminal prompt.
.PP
SSH authentication is handled by the system SSH client.
A password in an SSH URL is the SSH login password and requires
.BR sshpass (1);
it is not reused for a separate Xpra protocol challenge.
Agent or key authentication is recommended for graphical launches.
.PP
Passwords embedded in URLs may be exposed through command-line inspection or
shell history.
Prefer
.B XPRA_PASSWORD
for an Xpra protocol password and normal SSH key or agent authentication for
SSH.
.SH EXAMPLES
Connect to the default TCP port on the local host:
.PP
.RS
.B go-xpra tcp://127.0.0.1/
.RE
.PP
Trust a private certificate authority for a TLS connection:
.PP
.RS
.B go-xpra \-\-ssl\-ca\-cert /path/to/ca.pem ssl://server.example.com/
.RE
.PP
Connect through a secure WebSocket reverse proxy:
.PP
.RS
.B go-xpra wss://server.example.com/xpra/
.RE
.PP
Connect to display 100 through SSH:
.PP
.RS
.B go-xpra ssh://alice@server.example.com/100
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B go-xpra
exits with status 0 after a requested informational action or a clean session,
1 when a connection or runtime operation fails, and 2 for invalid command-line
usage.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR sshpass (1),
.BR xpra (1)
.PP
Project documentation:
.B https://github.com/Xpra-org/go-xpra
